# Particle Dissolve Button (`button-particle-dissolve`)

On click the button disintegrates: it's sampled into ~600 particles that drift away like sand in the wind (a left-to-right sweep), then a success pill springs in where it stood.

## What it should do
Build a particle-dissolve button: on click, measure the button, hide it, and draw ~650 particles sampled inside its pill shape on an oversized canvas; each particle starts moving after a delay proportional to its x (sweep), accelerates right and up with jitter and fades. When all are gone, show a success pill with a bouncy spring.

## Project requirements
- React 19 + TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, shadcn/ui-style CSS variables (`background`, `foreground`, `muted`, `muted-foreground`, `border`, `card`, `primary`, `ring`). If the project lacks shadcn, set it up first (`npx shadcn@latest init`).
- Animation engine: **canvas**.
- Install dependencies: `npm i motion lucide-react`
- Put the component at `components/kinetik/button-particle-dissolve.tsx`.
- `cn` lives at `lib/kinetik/utils.ts` (source below).
- Shared hooks go at `lib/kinetik/core.ts` (source below).
- Motion tokens go at `lib/kinetik/tokens.ts` (source below). Use tokens instead of magic numbers.

## Non-negotiables
- **Accessibility:** Success state is role=status; button disabled during the effect. Reduced motion: direct swap to success.
- **Performance:** Canvas only exists for ~2 s; particle count capped.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (replace movement with opacity or an instant change).
- Animate only transform / opacity / filter / clip-path. No hard-coded theme colors — use CSS variables so it works in light and dark.

